<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Anhalt-Bernburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1603 CE – 1863 CE · capital Bernburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Anhalt principality ruled from a castle above the Saale at Bernburg, whose line outlasted every other branch of the 1603 partition except Dessau.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt">Principality of Anhalt</a> was divided in 1603 among the heirs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joachim-ernest-prince-of-anhalt">Joachim Ernst</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christian-i-prince-of-anhalt-bernburg">Christian I</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a>. Christian was a leading Calvinist politician who directed the policy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-union">Protestant Union</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elector-palatine">Elector Palatine</a> before the defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">White Mountain</a> in 1620.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The state was small and agrarian, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> exclave around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ballenstedt">Ballenstedt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harzgerode">Harzgerode</a>. The ruling line was Calvinist. Branch lines split off, including Anhalt-Bernburg-Schaumburg-Hoym. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a> was raised to a duchy in 1806, with the court later moving partly to Ballenstedt.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last duke, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-karl-duke-of-anhalt-bernburg">Alexander Karl</a>, died childless in 1863, and his lands passed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt-dessau">Anhalt-Dessau</a>, which thus united all the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt">Anhalt</a> territories into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-anhalt">Duchy of Anhalt</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernburg">Bernburg</a> remained a district town within Anhalt.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-anhalt-bernburg.jpg" alt="Principality of Anhalt-Bernburg" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Germenfer · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_the_Anhalt_Duchies.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1603 CE – 1863 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bernburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Christian I, Victor Frederick, Alexis Frederick Christian</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Calvinism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Duchy of Anhalt-Bernburg, Anhalt-Bernburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1733&amp;focus=principality-of-anhalt-bernburg" aria-label="Principality of Anhalt-Bernburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4945 386 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Anhalt-Bernburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5265 642L5276 641M5265 642L5229 694M5265 642L5199 691M5265 642L5276 641"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5276 641h0M5229 694h0M5199 691h0M5276 641h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5265 642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5265 642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bernburg. <a href="/atlas?year=1733&amp;focus=principality-of-anhalt-bernburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
